 "beats": [
  {
   "id": "hook",
   "text": "She was the biggest blues star in America. Then she lay in an unmarked grave for thirty-three years.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "vv1936",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.0,
     1.06
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ]
   },
   "section": "hook",
   "overlays": {
    "hook": "The biggest blues star in America. *Then 33 years in an unmarked grave.*"
   },
   "captions": false,
   "speed": 1.3
  },
  {
   "id": "name",
   "text": "Her name was Bessie Smith. They called her the Empress of the Blues.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "close1",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.0,
     1.08
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ]
   },
   "section": "who"
  },
  {
   "id": "born",
   "text": "She was born in Chattanooga, Tennessee. Before she was nine, both of her parents were dead.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "young",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.05,
     1.15
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.42
    ]
   },
   "overlays": {
    "pin": {
     "label": "Chattanooga, Tennessee"
    }
   },
   "section": "story"
  },
  {
   "id": "busk",
   "text": "So she sang on the street corners for coins, while her brother Andrew played the guitar.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "vv72",
    "caption": "Bessie Smith, 1936"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "rainey",
   "text": "In 1912 she joined a traveling show as a dancer. The star singer in that company was Ma Rainey, the Mother of the Blues.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "rainey",
    "caption": "Ma Rainey, 1923"
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "1912"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "columbia",
   "text": "In 1923, Columbia Records signed her. Her first record, Downhearted Blues, sold about 780,000 copies in six months.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "ad1923",
    "caption": "Columbia Records advertisement, 1923"
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "1923"
   },
   "section": "fame"
  },
  {
   "id": "paid",
   "text": "She became the highest-paid Black entertainer of her day, earning up to two thousand dollars a week.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "bar8",
    "focus": [
     0.62,
     0.35
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.0,
     1.08
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "car",
   "text": "She toured the South in her own private railroad car.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "feathers",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.05,
     1.15
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.42
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "tent",
   "text": "In July 1927, she was singing in a tent in Concord, North Carolina. Hooded Klansmen came to pull the tent down.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "July 1927",
    "sub": "Concord, North Carolina"
   },
   "section": "klan"
  },
  {
   "id": "faced",
   "text": "Bessie walked out to them alone. She cursed them and told them to leave. They ran.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "argue",
    "focus": [
     0.3,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.0,
     1.06
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "finished",
   "text": "Then she went back inside and finished the show.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "bar3",
    "focus": [
     0.62,
     0.35
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.0,
     1.08
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "film",
   "text": "In 1929 she made her only film, St. Louis Blues. This is it.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "poster",
    "caption": "St. Louis Blues, 1929"
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "1929"
   },
   "section": "film"
  },
  {
   "id": "film2",
   "text": "She played a woman left by the man she loved, singing her heart out at a bar.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "close2",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.0,
     1.08
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "crash1",
   "text": "Then the Depression hit. Record sales collapsed, and in 1931 Columbia let her go. She had to sell her railroad car.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "floor",
    "focus": [
     0.35,
     0.45
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.0,
     1.06
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.5
    ]
   },
   "section": "fall"
  },
  {
   "id": "comeback",
   "text": "By the mid-1930s she was fighting her way back, singing swing in New York.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "bar12",
    "focus": [
     0.62,
     0.35
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.0,
     1.08
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "crash",
   "text": "On September 26th, 1937, she was riding down Highway 61 in Mississippi. Her car slammed into the back of a truck.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "September 26, 1937",
    "sub": "Highway 61, Mississippi"
   },
   "section": "crash"
  },
  {
   "id": "arm",
   "text": "Her right arm was almost torn off. She was taken to the Black hospital in Clarksdale, and died that morning.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "vv75",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.25
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.05,
     1.15
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "myth",
   "text": "For years, people said a white hospital had turned her away. That story was false. But what came next was true.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "A myth",
    "sub": "a white hospital did not turn her away"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "funeral",
   "text": "In Philadelphia, ten thousand people filed past her coffin.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "photo",
    "image": "vv1936",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.3
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.15,
     1.25
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.42
    ]
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "October 1937"
   },
   "section": "grave"
  },
  {
   "id": "nostone",
   "text": "But her grave got no headstone. Her estranged husband, Jack Gee, reportedly pocketed money raised to buy one.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "No headstone",
    "sub": "Mount Lawn Cemetery, Pennsylvania"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "years",
   "text": "For thirty-three years, the Empress of the Blues lay in an unmarked grave.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "close3",
    "focus": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.0,
     1.08
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.4
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "joplin",
   "text": "Then in 1970, the rock star Janis Joplin, who idolized her, split the cost of a stone with Juanita Green, who had done housework for Bessie as a girl.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "joplin",
    "caption": "Janis Joplin, 1970"
   },
   "overlays": {
    "stamp": "August 1970"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "stone",
   "text": "It reads: The greatest blues singer in the world will never stop singing.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "card",
    "image": "grave",
    "caption": "Bessie Smith's headstone, Mount Lawn Cemetery"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "janis",
   "text": "Two months later, Janis Joplin was dead too.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "title",
    "title": "October 1970",
    "sub": "Janis Joplin dies at 27"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "close",
   "text": "That was Bessie Smith.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "video",
    "clip": "bar14",
    "focus": [
     0.62,
     0.35
    ],
    "zoom": [
     1.0,
     1.08
    ],
    "anchor": [
     0.5,
     0.35
    ]
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "cta",
   "text": "Follow The Black History Room for more.",
   "visual": {
    "kind": "endcard"
   },
   "section": "cta",
   "captions": false
  }
 ],
